    "title": "[0709.1273v1] (GL(n+1,R),GL(n,R)) is a Gelfand pair",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# Mathematics > Representation Theory\n\n  [Submitted on 9 Sep 2007 (this version), \n\n    *latest version 17 May 2009*(v4)]\n# Title:(GL(n+1,R),GL(n,R)) is a Gelfand pair\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n            Abstract:  Consider the standard embedding of GL(n,R) to GL(n+1,R) and the two-sided action of GL(n,R) \\times GL(n,R) on GL(n+1,R).\n\nIn this paper we show that any GL(n,R) \\times GL(n,R)-invariant distribution on GL(n+1,R) is invariant with respect to transposition.\n\nWe show that this implies that the pair (GL(n+1,R),GL(n,R)) is a Gelfand pair. Namely, for any irreducible admissible continuous representation (\\pi,E) of GL(n+1,R) on a Hilbert space E, dimHom_{GL(n,R)}(E^{\\infty},C) \\leq 1. We also prove an analogous result for the pair (GL(n+1,C),GL(n,C)).\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Dmitry Gourevitch [view email]\n**[v1]**Sun, 9 Sep 2007 16:59:20 UTC (33 KB)\n\n**[v2]**Mon, 1 Oct 2007 09:03:23 UTC (37 KB)\n\n**[v3]**Tue, 22 Apr 2008 15:25:35 UTC (24 KB)\n\n**[v4]**Sun, 17 May 2009 11:47:14 UTC (24 KB)\n\n### References & Citations\n\n    \n    Loading...\n\n# Bibliographic and Citation Tools\n\n            Bibliographic Explorer \n\n        *(What is the Explorer?)*\n            Connected Papers \n\n        *(What is Connected Papers?)*\n            Litmaps \n\n        *(What is Litmaps?)*\n            scite Smart Citations \n\n        *(What are Smart Citations?)*\n# Code, Data and Media Associated with this Article\n\n            alphaXiv \n\n        *(What is alphaXiv?)*\n            CatalyzeX Code Finder for Papers \n\n        *(What is CatalyzeX?)*\n            DagsHub \n\n        *(What is DagsHub?)*\n            Gotit.pub \n\n        *(What is GotitPub?)*\n            Hugging Face \n\n        *(What is Huggingface?)*\n            ScienceCast \n\n        *(What is ScienceCast?)*\n# Demos\n\n# Recommenders and Search Tools\n\n              Influence Flower \n\n          *(What are Influence Flowers?)*\n              CORE Recommender \n\n          *(What is CORE?)*\n# arXivLabs: experimental projects with community collaborators\n\narXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\n\nBoth individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\n\nHave an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? **Learn more about arXivLabs**.",
